because of climate change mountain  glaciers are melting rapidly and  affecting numerous ecosystems but  getting up close readings on these  high-altitude environments is difficult  satellite images don't have enough  detail and can be easily thwarted by  clouds airplanes can map glaciers in  high resolution but the costs can be  astronomical Oliver Whitmore a scientist  at the University of Colorado has  developed a new high-flying drone that  can take images at altitudes of up to  6,000 meters above sea level  normally drones have trouble reaching  these Heights because the air is too  thin for their propellers to build up  enough left but with this new design  weight is kept to a minimum  the tiny aircraft only weighs 2  kilograms and with six individual rotors  each over a foot long generating lift  shouldn't be a problem to give their UAV  a shakedown cruise researchers took it  to the heights of the Andes Mountains in  South America where 70% of the Earth's  tropical ice is located at the melting  glaciers are often what supplies fresh  water to communities in the summer  with the drones help researchers are  able to get close-up pictures and create  detailed models of the glacier surface  using drone surveys collected one year  apart scientists are able to see where  and how quickly the glacier ice is  melting while the flights through the  Andes were the first test of the UAV it  was only the first stop on a world tour  the researchers are taking it to even  greater heights in the Andes and are  using this kind of close-up data  gathering for other environmental  studies in places like the mountains of  Colorado  [Music]  [Music]
 

